Run meson under `sys.executable` so pandas' meson-python build reads the
target Python's sysconfig instead of the build host's.

meson-python does not import meson; it starts it as a separate process. Left
alone it resolves the bare name `meson` on PATH, and the console script it
finds there carries a shebang naming the build interpreter. meson therefore
runs under the build Python and answers every question about "the Python we
are building for" from that interpreter's own sysconfig, so the include
directory it hands the compiler for the Cython sanity check is the build
host's rather than the cross environment's. On 64-bit targets the two headers
happen to agree and nothing is noticed; on the 32-bit Android ABIs
(armeabi-v7a and x86) the host's `pyport.h` was written for an 8-byte pointer
and the first compile stops at

    pyport.h: LONG_BIT definition appears wrong for platform

so those two slices die while arm64-v8a and iOS build cleanly. That split is
the tell: it is an interpreter mix-up, not anything pandas does differently
on 32-bit.

The fix is one change spread over two files, and neither half is any use
without the other:

  1. `pyproject.toml` gains `[tool.meson-python] meson = "meson-wrapper.py"`.
     meson-python special-cases a value ending in `.py`: rather than
     executing it, it runs `[sys.executable, <value>]`. Under forge's
     cross-build that `sys.executable` is the cross-python wrapper, which
     exports `_PYTHON_SYSCONFIGDATA_NAME`, so meson and everything it starts
     see the target's sysconfig. The value is resolved against the build's
     working directory and must exist there, which is why it stays a bare
     relative filename.

  2. `meson-wrapper.py` is that script, added at the root of the unpacked
     sdist next to `pyproject.toml`. It hands straight over to
     `mesonbuild.mesonmain.main()` and adds no behaviour of its own; its
     entire purpose is to be a Python file rather than a console script, so
     that the choice of interpreter belongs to meson-python instead of to a
     shebang baked in when meson was installed.

The change has one visible trace on device: pandas ships a copy of its own
`pyproject.toml` inside the wheel, so the installed file carries the added
table and differs from upstream's by exactly those lines.

--- a/pyproject.toml
+++ b/pyproject.toml
@@ -833,3 +833,15 @@
 [tool.codespell]
 ignore-words-list = "blocs, coo, hist, nd, sav, ser, recuse, nin, timere, expec, expecs, indext, SME, NotIn, tructures, tru, indx, abd, ABD"
 ignore-regex = 'https://([\w/\.])+'
+
+# Added by mobile-forge mobile.patch — see meson-wrapper.py.
+# Pointing `meson` at a Python script forces meson-python to invoke meson
+# through sys.executable, which under forge's cross-build is the
+# cross-Python wrapper. Without this, meson-python uses the bare `meson`
+# CLI from PATH, which resolves to a shebang under the BUILD Python — and
+# meson then queries the BUILD Python's sysconfig, leaking the host
+# Python's Python.h include path into the cython sanity check. On 32-bit
+# Android targets this surfaces as
+# `pyport.h: LONG_BIT definition appears wrong for platform`.
+[tool.meson-python]
+meson = "meson-wrapper.py"
--- /dev/null	2026-05-31 12:00:00
+++ b/meson-wrapper.py	2026-05-31 12:00:00
@@ -0,0 +1,12 @@
+"""Forwards to the meson CLI through `sys.executable` so meson-python
+invokes meson under the same Python interpreter the build is running
+under. For forge's cross-build that's the cross-Python wrapper, which
+sets _PYTHON_SYSCONFIGDATA_NAME so meson queries the TARGET sysconfig
+rather than the BUILD/host one. Without this the cython sanity check
+sees the host Python.h's SIZEOF_VOID_P=8 on 32-bit Android targets
+(armeabi-v7a, x86) and explodes."""
+
+import sys
+from mesonbuild.mesonmain import main
+
+sys.exit(main())
